/* Logic: 
1. Initially, count is 0 because we haven't found any 1s yet.

2. Keep checking whether n is greater than 0. If it is, continue the loop.

3. Increase count by 1 because we are going to remove one set bit (1).

4. Use n AND (n - 1) to remove the rightmost set bit (1) from n.

5. Repeat the same process until all the set bits are removed and n becomes 0.

6. Once n becomes 0, the loop stops, and we return count, which gives us the total number of 1s in the binary representation.

Remember: In every iteration, we remove exactly one 1 and increase the count by 1.
*/

class Solution {
public:
    int hammingWeight(int n) {
        int count = 0; // Stores the number of set bits (1s)

        while (n > 0) { // Continue until all set bits are removed
            count++;    // Increment count for each set bit

            // Removes the rightmost set bit (1) from n
            n = n & (n - 1);
        }

        return count; // Return the total number of set bits
    }
};
